| 2 | 32 | 7 | 0.218750000 | 6.944444444444e-01 |
| 4 | 32 | 3 | 0.093750000 | 4.822530864198e-01 |
| 8 | 32 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 2.325680393614e-01 |
| 16 | 32 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 5.408789293240e-02 |
| 32 | 32 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 2.925500161866e-03 |
| 64 | 32 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 8.558551197080e-06 |
| 128 | 32 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 7.324879859303e-11 |
\image(figures/karger_profile.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Finite seeded failures are separated from the ideal-random probability bound.)
\alttext(The plotted seed census is an observed fraction; the second curve is a mathematical bound under ideal independence, not a calibrated confidence interval.)

The default zero trial argument selects the legacy n² ceil(log n) schedule with
checked products. Positive budgets run every trial. Graph endpoints and complete
n+m/trial ceilings are checked before graph copies or union-find allocation.
Trace defaults admit a million input records, a million trials and a million
online history records; zero disables the corresponding optional cap. A
contraction or completed-trial snapshot charges n+1 records before its captured
operation. Capacities, bytes and peak RAM are excluded. Failures expose no
partial result.

Ranked path-compressed union-find gives expected word work O(B(n+(n+1)m
alpha(n))) for this repeated full-edge-scan implementation, with O(n+m)
working/output storage. Capturing n labels per contraction/trial adds up to
quadratic-per-trial owned records and associated observation work; it is not a
free trace. Random draw counts include rejection words, while edge-check
counters exclude validation, union-find internals and history copies.

Independent exact cut enumeration checked all 64 four-vertex simple graphs,
1,024 trial partitions and 1,920 actual contractions, including candidate ranks,
accepted-word modulo mapping and canonical component labels. A further 1,024
seeded studies matched the frozen original partitions and cut sizes when the
ordinary draws required no rejection. These finite checks do not turn a
randomized candidate into an optimality certificate. The shared ten-report build
passed 131 native tests, 38 directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour
sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public examples, documentation coverage for all 388
scoped definitions and ten representative clangd hovers. All four complete C++
report programs executed successfully and their native figures were visually
reviewed. Batch receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> Reference

David R. Karger (1993), “Global Min-cuts in RNC, and Other Ramifications of a
Simple Min-Cut Algorithm”, SODA,21–30;
[author's 1992 preprint|https://people.csail.mit.edu/karger/Papers/mincut.pdf].
This report implements the simple unweighted multigraph contraction process, not
the later recursive Karger–Stein algorithm.
